WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.619
In this video, I'm going to walk you through the basic steps behind how to make a chunk.

2
00:00:11.619 --> 00:00:14.020
Every discipline is a little different.

3
00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:18.100
Chunking in the subject of history, for example, is quite different from chunking in chemistry

4
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.020
or in karate.

5
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:24.739
In my explanations here, I'm going to lean a little more towards explaining chunking

6
00:00:24.739 --> 00:00:28.500
of mental ideas rather than physical body motions.

7
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.680
But you'll see that the two approaches are closely related, so whether you're learning

8
00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:38.180
something mental or something physical, you'll find some helpful ideas here.

9
00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:43.900
The first step on chunking is simply to focus your undivided attention on the information

10
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:45.860
you want to chunk.

11
00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:49.819
If you have the television going on in the background or you're looking up every few

12
00:00:49.819 --> 00:00:55.259
minutes to check or answer your phone or computer messages, it means you're going to have

13
00:00:55.259 --> 00:01:01.099
more difficulty in making a chunk because your brain is not really focusing on chunking

14
00:01:01.099 --> 00:01:03.220
the new material.

15
00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:08.220
When you first begin to learn something, you're making new neural patterns and connecting

16
00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:13.379
them with pre-existing patterns that are spread through many areas of the brain.

17
00:01:13.379 --> 00:01:19.419
Your octopus tentacles, so to speak, can't reach very well if some of them are off on

18
00:01:19.419 --> 00:01:24.699
other thoughts, using up some of the limited slots in your working memory.

19
00:01:24.699 --> 00:01:30.660
The second step in chunking is to understand the basic idea you're trying to chunk, whether

20
00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:36.540
it's understanding a concept such as continental drift, seeing the connection between the basic

21
00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:43.319
elements of a plot for a story, grasping the economic principle of supply and demand, or

22
00:01:43.319 --> 00:01:48.099
comprehending the essence of a particular type of math problem.

23
00:01:48.099 --> 00:01:54.940
Students can often synthesize the gist, that is, figure out the main idea or ideas, pretty

24
00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:56.139
naturally.

25
00:01:56.139 --> 00:02:01.459
Or at least, they can grasp those ideas if they allow the focused and diffuse modes of

26
00:02:01.459 --> 00:02:06.699
thinking to take turns in helping them figure out what's going on.

27
00:02:06.699 --> 00:02:12.360
Understanding is like a superglue that helps hold the underlying memory traces together.

28
00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:18.199
It creates broad, encompassing traces that can link to other memory traces.

29
00:02:18.199 --> 00:02:20.919
Can you create a chunk if you don't understand?

30
00:02:20.919 --> 00:02:26.720
Yes, but it's often a useless chunk that won't fit in with or relate to other material

31
00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:28.720
you're learning.

32
00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:35.080
That said, it's important to realize that just understanding how a problem was solved,

33
00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:42.600
for example, does not necessarily create a chunk that you can easily call to mind later.

34
00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:49.399
Don't confuse the aha of a breakthrough in understanding with solid expertise.

35
00:02:49.399 --> 00:02:54.199
That's part of why you can grasp an idea when a teacher presents it in class.

36
00:02:54.199 --> 00:03:00.080
But if you don't review it fairly soon after you've first learned it, it can seem incomprehensible

37
00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:03.440
when it comes time to prepare for a test.

38
00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:09.119
In math and science-related subjects, closing the book and testing yourself on whether you

39
00:03:09.119 --> 00:03:14.839
yourself can solve the problem you think you understand will speed up your learning at

40
00:03:14.839 --> 00:03:15.839
this stage.

41
00:03:15.839 --> 00:03:21.500
You often realize the first time you actually understand something is when you can actually

42
00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.080
do it yourself.

43
00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:25.119
It's the same in many disciplines.

44
00:03:25.119 --> 00:03:29.119
Just looking at someone else's painting doesn't mean you could actually create that

45
00:03:29.119 --> 00:03:34.440
painting yourself, and just hearing a song won't give you the expertise you need to

46
00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:43.960
sing it in the same resonant fashion.

47
00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:49.039
Just because you see it, or even that you understand it, it doesn't mean that you

48
00:03:49.039 --> 00:03:51.279
can actually do it.

49
00:03:51.279 --> 00:03:58.440
Only doing it yourself helps create the neural patterns that underlie true mastery.

50
00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:04.600
The third step to chunking is gaining context so you can see not just how, but also when

51
00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:07.119
to use this chunk.

52
00:04:07.119 --> 00:04:12.479
Context means going beyond the initial problem and seeing more broadly, repeating and practicing

53
00:04:12.479 --> 00:04:17.880
with both related and unrelated problems so that you can see not only when to use the

54
00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:20.899
chunk, but when not to use it.

55
00:04:20.899 --> 00:04:25.760
This helps you see how your newly formed chunk fits into the bigger picture.

56
00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:31.079
In other words, you may have a tool in your strategy or problem-solving toolbox, but if

57
00:04:31.079 --> 00:04:35.799
you don't know when to use that tool, it's not going to do you a lot of good.

58
00:04:35.799 --> 00:04:41.720
Ultimately, practice helps you broaden the networks of neurons that are connected to

59
00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:49.000
your chunk, ensuring it's not only firm, but also accessible from many different paths.

60
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:56.880
As you can see from this top-down, bottom-up illustration, learning takes place in two ways.

61
00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:02.519
There's a bottom-up chunking process where practice and repetition can help you both

62
00:05:02.519 --> 00:05:09.279
build and strengthen each chunk so you can easily access it whenever you need to.

63
00:05:09.279 --> 00:05:15.440
And there's also a sort of a top-down, big-picture process that allows you to see what you're

64
00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:18.119
learning and where it fits in.

65
00:05:18.119 --> 00:05:23.880
Both processes are vital in gaining mastery over the material.

66
00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:28.519
Context is where bottom-up and top-down learning meet.

67
00:05:28.519 --> 00:05:35.720
To clarify here, chunking may involve your learning how to use a certain problem-solving technique.

68
00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:43.239
Context means learning when to use that technique instead of some other technique.

69
00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:49.480
Doing a rapid two-minute picture walk through a chapter in a book before you begin studying it,

70
00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:55.880
glancing at pictures and section headings, can allow you to gain a sense of the big picture.

71
00:05:55.880 --> 00:06:00.040
So can listening to a very well-organized lecture.

72
00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:05.160
These kinds of activities can help you know where to put the chunks you're constructing,

73
00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:12.119
how the chunks relate to one another, just as you see here with the image of the man in the car.

74
00:06:12.119 --> 00:06:15.000
Learn the major concepts or points first.

75
00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:20.119
These are often the key parts of a good instructor or book chapters outline,

76
00:06:20.119 --> 00:06:23.399
flowcharts, tables, or concept maps.

77
00:06:23.399 --> 00:06:27.239
Once you have this done, fill in the details.

78
00:06:27.239 --> 00:06:31.399
Even if a few of the puzzle pieces are missing at the end of your studies,

79
00:06:31.399 --> 00:06:34.440
you can still see the big picture.

80
00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:35.799
So there you go.

81
00:06:35.799 --> 00:06:39.559
Summing it up, chunks are best built with

82
00:06:39.559 --> 00:06:41.480
focused attention,

83
00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:43.959
understanding of the basic idea,

84
00:06:43.959 --> 00:06:49.239
and practice to help you gain mastery and a sense of the big picture context.

85
00:06:49.239 --> 00:06:52.279
Those are the essential steps in making a chunk

86
00:06:52.279 --> 00:06:58.119
and fitting that chunk into a greater conceptual overview of what you're learning.

87
00:06:58.119 --> 00:06:59.880
But there's more.

88
00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:01.559
I'm Barbara Oakley.

89
00:07:01.559 --> 00:07:11.239
Thanks for learning how to learn.